#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sable-screen — the manager pre-dispatch/pre-close safety screens,
mechanized (SABLE-23upx).

CONTEXT
-------
The optimus shift report (SABLE-1eeg7 sections 3 and 4a) carries two
mandatory screens as PROSE ALGORITHMS that every manager recycle re-authors
from scratch: a pre-dispatch collision check and a pre-close hold sweep. The
algorithm is durable; the implementation is not, and each re-implementation
is a fresh chance to get it wrong. It already has: the first hold-sweep
re-author used bare `git rev-parse origin/<branch>`, which ECHOES the
literal ref string back to stdout for a ref that does not exist (failure
shows up only in the exit status / stderr), so every branch that had already
landed and been reaped resolved to a truthy, plausible-looking string. The
sweep reported 300 of 309 closed beads as NEEDS HOLD while simultaneously
printing "landed: 0" — caught only because that number contradicted two
facts (SABLE-be4lo.1, SABLE-be4lo.6) the operator had independently verified
minutes earlier. A screen that reports 300 findings and one that reports 6
look equally "working" without an independent fact to check the output
against.

CONTRACT
--------
dispatch <bead-id> [<bead-id> ...]
  The screen-v2 collision check. Occupant set is built TWO ways — a
  declaration-only screen is blind to the second:
    (a) every OTHER in_progress bead's declared writes (metadata.wip_claims /
        metadata.footprint_writes, read DIRECTLY — see "NO PROSE FALLBACK").
    (b) every origin wk-* branch NOT contained in the integration branch,
        using its ACTUAL git-diffed changed files, never any declaration
        (SABLE-krbxd: this is the only leg that catches undeclared
        collateral edits — a declaration-only screen cannot see them).
  Each candidate's own declared writes are checked against the union of both
  occupant sets, excluding the candidates themselves from leg (a). A
  candidate that declares no footprint at all gets the distinct
  NO-DECLARATION verdict (SABLE-e2ic3 / SABLE-tm8nl: 96.4% of the open pool
  declares nothing, and "checked, found no overlap" must stay
  distinguishable from "nothing to check with") — it is neither CLEAR nor
  COLLIDES, and it still dispatches: a screen that can never release a
  no-declaration bead is exactly as broken as one that can never deny a real
  collision.
    (c) SIBLING LEG (SABLE-1muvg). Excluding the candidates from leg (a) is
        right for leg (a)'s purpose — a bead must not collide with itself —
        but for two releases NOTHING re-added candidate-vs-candidate
        comparison, so a multi-bead invocation screened each candidate
        against the WORLD and never against its siblings. A manager passes N
        ids for exactly one reason (can these go out in parallel?), so
        all-CLEAR read as co-dispatch approved: `dispatch SABLE-su3j3
        SABLE-xgb29` returned CLEAR/CLEAR for BYTE-IDENTICAL declared sets,
        the maximal-overlap case. Every PAIR of candidates is now compared —
        all pairs, not each-against-the-first — and an overlapping pair gets
        its own SIBLING-COLLISION verdict naming both bead ids and the
        shared paths, distinct from both CLEAR and COLLIDES because the
        remedy is different (bundle onto one worktree, or serialize —
        neither of which is what COLLIDES asks for). With N=1 the leg cannot
        fire and single-arg output is unchanged.

  READINESS AXIS (SABLE-qyr0f), reported ALONGSIDE the footprint verdict and
  never folded into it. The three verdicts above answer "does this collide on
  files?" while the caller is asking "is this dispatchable?" (the SABLE-1eeg7
  §9 instrument-reach hazard), and a BLOCKED bead declaring only NEW files is
  both maximally likely to screen CLEAR and exactly the shape of a
  later-in-the-chain bead: SABLE-21rug.3 and SABLE-21rug.6 both screened a
  clean ✓ while blocked by an open sibling. Readiness is resolved from two
  sources with different precisions:
    - the candidate's OPEN 'blocks' dependencies, read from its own `bd show
      --json` record (which carries each dep's resolved status) — this is the
      precise signal, it names the blocker ids, and it DENIES.
    - membership in `bd ready --limit 0` — bd's own definition of
      dispatchable. Absence is the fuzzy signal: an in_progress or closed
      bead is also absent, so absence alone means "not dispatchable now",
      NOT "blocked by a dependency". It is REPORTED (not-ready, naming the
      status) and does not deny, because turning an ambiguous signal into a
      deny inside the very tool whose bug class is "answered a narrower
      question than the one asked" is the same mistake with the sign
      flipped. Measured against this repo's store: 21 open beads with an
      open 'blocks' dep, ALL 21 absent from bd ready, zero blocked-but-ready
      — the precise leg agrees with bd and never over-denies.
  blocked-by-dependency and collides-on-files are different facts with
  different remedies; a candidate that is both reports both.

holds
  The hold sweep. Any CLOSED bead whose metadata.branch still exists on
  origin AND is NOT contained in the integration branch MUST carry all four
  hold fields (hold, hold_by, hold_since, hold_until) — SABLE-zvx4i, the
  stranded-branch class a missing hold makes invisible to the ready pool,
  the lane record, and the merge seat all at once.
  THE FIX: containment is decided from `git rev-parse --verify --quiet
  "origin/<branch>^{commit}"`, whose EMPTY stdout means "no such ref" and
  whose non-empty stdout is a real commit SHA. A branch with no ref on
  origin is reported as reaped-or-unpushed, never as needing a hold — this
  tool exists so that failure mode is structurally avoided rather than
  merely documented in a shift report.

  BRANCH-SCOPED, NOT BEAD-SCOPED (SABLE-jhako). The sweep's unit of concern
  is the BRANCH — it keys on metadata.branch existing on origin and not
  being contained in the integration ref — but the hold FIELDS live on the
  bead, so with N beads bundled onto one branch, N-1 could be silently
  unheld. wk-recycle-handoff carried SABLE-vsfvl + SABLE-uc7kh; chuck placed
  a full four-field hold on uc7kh and one run reported BOTH "NEEDS HOLD:
  SABLE-vsfvl wk-recycle-handoff" AND "SABLE-uc7kh held-ok" — one branch,
  two verdicts, each truthful about its bead and contradictory about the
  branch. Both directions are damaging: a seat reading vsfvl may promote a
  branch chuck holds for three reasons, and the NEEDS-HOLD line is a false
  alarm that trains readers to discount the sweep (the SABLE-ijd7j
  false-fallback dynamic that made the durable inbox write-only). Rows are
  therefore emitted PER BRANCH, exactly once, listing every bead on it: a
  branch is HELD when ANY of its beads carries all four fields, and the
  NEEDS-HOLD line reports what is still missing on the best-covered bead. A
  single-bead branch is unchanged — its row is its bead's row.

activation <bead-id> ... | --ready
  THE LANDABILITY SCREEN (SABLE-6eu9w). Every gate above answers "will this
  bead COLLIDE with something in flight?"; NOTHING answered "if this bead
  succeeds, CAN THE RESULT ACTUALLY LAND?" — so a manager could dispatch work
  that is perfectly scoped, perfectly disjoint, correctly implemented, fully
  verified, and then structurally unmergeable. Measured 2026-07-26: of 43
  installed sable bins, 39 were LIVE SYMLINKS into the working tree, so the
  pull IS the activation and ~90% of bin/ work is verify-but-hold under the
  standing operator-present hot-swap regime. Three finished, well-evidenced
  branches were correctly held that night for activation shape rather than for
  any defect; the dispatch decisions that produced them were made blind.

  Landability is decided ENTIRELY by the activation shape of the files in the
  footprint, which is knowable BEFORE dispatch from the declared footprint
  alone. Each declared path classifies as:
    HOT-SWAP        an installed artifact resolves INTO a live working tree of
                    this repo. Landing ACTIVATES. Operator-present event.
    HOT-SWAP-       not installed itself, but imported/sourced by a hot-swap
      CLOSURE       artifact beside it, so the pull activates it all the same.
                    A symlink activates its whole import closure, not just the
                    file it names — see ClosureIndex.
    PIN-MANAGED     installed as a symlink resolving OUTSIDE every live tree
                    (a pinned snapshot). Lands inert; a re-pin activates.
    COPY-INSTALLED  installed as a real file. Lands inert; an install
                    activates, and that obligation has no ledger (SABLE-3zjc1).
    INERT           nothing installed corresponds to it (tests, CI scripts,
                    docs). Lands and takes effect with no activation step.
  The BEAD verdict is the WORST CASE across its paths — one hot-swap path holds
  the whole branch.

  THE DERIVATION IS MECHANICAL, NEVER A LIST. The install state is resolved at
  screen time by walking the install roots and realpath()-ing each artifact
  against `git worktree list`. A hand-maintained catalogue of which bins are
  symlinks is the SABLE-hadqx failure this fleet keeps repeating (SABLE-ev5i5
  three resolver mirrors, SABLE-e535u a hand-enumerated lane, SABLE-r69ho
  hardcoded pane ids in a stall probe that therefore could not detect a stall).
  An install layout that CAN change WILL change. Only the install ROOTS — where
  installers write — are configured, and they are overridable.

  IT MUST NOT BE NOISY and IT MUST NOT GUESS, in that order of temptation. A
  fully-inert footprint produces NO warning and exits 0; a screen that warns on
  every dispatch gets ignored, which is worse than absent (the SABLE-r5pfw
  erosion). But a token it cannot classify is COULD-NOT-ASSESS, NEVER inert —
  INERT is a positive claim ("this activates on nothing"), and defaulting to it
  fails PERMISSIVE. That direction is not hypothetical: the hand-run this bead
  replaces decided path-ness by testing for a slash, and SABLE-p0grx declares
  the token `wk-runlock-instrument/land).` — a phantom WITH a slash, which
  matches no installed artifact and so classified INERT, pushing the bead
  TOWARD the landable list. A declared path that does not exist in the repo is
  therefore COULD-NOT-ASSESS (phantom or to-be-created, and both need saying
  out loud), never INERT. 19 of 67 open beads carrying a footprint section
  parsed to phantoms at the time this was specified — dirty input is the
  normal case, not the anomaly.

  THE SECTION TRICHOTOMY IS PRESERVED: ABSENT ("declares nothing" — legitimate,
  dispatches normally, no warning), PRESENT-BUT-UNREADABLE (prose that yields
  no path token — e.g. writing "(none — ...)" inside the section, which parses
  to the tokens "(none" and "it"), and POPULATED are three inputs, not two.

  TOKENIZATION IS BORROWED, NOT REBUILT — see _footprint_section() below.

NO PROSE FALLBACK IN dispatch/holds (intentional — do not add one)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Both of those screens read metadata.wip_claims / metadata.footprint_writes and STOP.
An earlier prototype of the dispatch screen fell back to parsing a bead's
'## File footprint' prose section when metadata was absent, and that
fallback manufactured junk tokens ("bin/sable-spawn-worker's",
"bin/sable-merge-gate:295") that do not exist anywhere in the stored
claims — a false "the corpus is corrupted" signal that was actually the
parser's own artifact (same class as SABLE-r5pfw: mentioning something is
not the same as it being true). A bead with no structured declaration gets
NO-DECLARATION here, never a best-effort guess reconstructed from prose.

`activation` DOES read the prose section, and that is not an inconsistency:
the two screens fail in opposite directions on a junk token. A junk token in a
COLLISION screen manufactures a false overlap — a deny nobody can act on. The
same token in a LANDABILITY screen matches no installed artifact, so treating
it as a path would manufacture a false LANDABLE. `activation` therefore
consumes the prose section but classifies an unrecognised token, and a path
absent from the repo, as COULD-NOT-ASSESS rather than as a real path or as
inert — which turns the junk into an honest non-answer instead of either kind
of false verdict.

LIMIT 0
-------
Every `bd list` / `bd ready` call passes --limit 0 explicitly (SABLE-52aym: a
bare `bd list` truncates at 50 SILENTLY, and a truncated occupant set,
closed-bead set, or READY set fails in the RELEASING direction — exactly the
direction a safety screen must never fail in. A truncated ready set in
particular would make blocked and ready indistinguishable, recreating
SABLE-qyr0f inside its own fix).

`bd list --status closed` keeps its explicit status filter: a bare `bd list`
returns open/deferred/in_progress/blocked and NO closed beads at all
(measured: 556 rows, zero closed), so dropping the filter would silently
sweep nothing. The filter is also never REPEATED — `bd list` keeps only the
last of a repeated --status and drops the rest silently (SABLE-4t97d).

Usage:
  sable-screen dispatch <bead-id> [<bead-id> ...] [--repo DIR] [--format text|json]
  sable-screen holds [--repo DIR] [--format text|json]
  sable-screen activation [<bead-id> ...] [--ready] [--install-root DIR]...
                          [--repo DIR] [--format text|json]

Exit codes:
  dispatch:   0 every candidate is dispatchable (clear/no-declaration, and
              not blocked), 1 at least one COLLIDES, SIBLING-COLLISION, or
              BLOCKED, 2 a candidate bead id could not be read at all.
  holds:      0 no NEEDS-HOLD findings, 1 at least one.
  activation: 0 every bead is landable (or declares nothing), 1 at least one
              HOLD-EXPECTED, 2 at least one COULD-NOT-ASSESS or unreadable
              bead id. 2 outranks 1: "the screen could not do its job" is a
              different call to action from "the screen did its job and the
              answer is no", and the louder code belongs to the one a reader
              must not silently treat as answered.

Env overrides (test seams, mirroring SABLE_CONTAINED_GIT / SABLE_MG_GIT):
  SABLE_SCREEN_GIT             git binary/wrapper (default: git)
  SABLE_SCREEN_BD              bd binary/wrapper (default: bd)
  SABLE_SCREEN_INSTALL_ROOTS   os.pathsep-separated install roots for
                               `activation` (default: see DEFAULT install
                               roots below). Set to a scratch prefix in tests
                               — SABLE-2avau records an installer silently
                               targeting the live ~/.claude and SABLE-k0nvp a
                               pinning suite polluting the real ~/.local twice.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))

import sable_gate_git_lib as git_lib  # noqa: E402

HOLD_FIELDS = ("hold", "hold_by", "hold_since", "hold_until")

# dispatch verdicts (the footprint axis)
NO_DECLARATION = "no-declaration"
CLEAR = "clear"
COLLIDES = "collides"
SIBLING_COLLISION = "sibling-collision"

# dispatch readiness (a SECOND axis, reported alongside — never folded into
# the footprint verdict: blocked-by-dependency and collides-on-files are
# different facts with different remedies, SABLE-qyr0f)
READY = "ready"
BLOCKED = "blocked"
NOT_READY = "not-ready"
READINESS_UNKNOWN = "readiness-unknown"

# Only 'blocks' edges gate readiness. 'relates-to' (442 of 538 edges in this
# repo's store) and 'parent-child' do NOT: counting them would report this
# tool's own beads as blocked by every bead anyone ever cross-referenced.
BLOCKING_DEP_TYPES = frozenset({"blocks"})
# A dep whose status is missing from the record is treated as still blocking:
# an unreadable blocker must fail toward the deny, never toward the release.
CLOSED_DEP_STATUSES = frozenset({"closed"})

# hold-sweep verdicts
NEEDS_HOLD = "needs-hold"
HELD_OK = "held-ok"
LANDED_CLEAN = "landed"
LANDED_STALE_HOLD = "landed-stale-hold"
REAPED_OR_UNPUSHED = "reaped-or-unpushed"
UNRESOLVED = "unresolved"


# --- process boundary --------------------------------------------------------

def _git(repo: str, *args: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Never raises: a missing/misconfigured git is an UNRESOLVED verdict
    here, not a traceback. Overridable via SABLE_SCREEN_GIT so tests can
    inject a faulty git for fault-injection coverage (mirrors
    sable-contained's SABLE_CONTAINED_GIT seam)."""
    tool = os.environ.get("SABLE_SCREEN_GIT", "git").split()
    try:
        return subprocess.run(tool + list(args), cwd=repo, text=True,
                              capture_output=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(list(args), 127, "", str(exc))


def _bd(repo: str, *args: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    tool = os.environ.get("SABLE_SCREEN_BD", "bd").split()
    try:
        return subprocess.run(tool + list(args), cwd=repo, text=True,
                              capture_output=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(list(args), 127, "", str(exc))


def _bd_json(repo: str, *args: str) -> list | None:
    """`bd ... --json` -> a list of records, or None on any failure (bad
    exit, empty stdout, unparseable JSON). Callers treat None exactly like
    "nothing came back" — never as a silent empty-list success, since a
    caller that cannot tell "zero records" from "bd is broken" is how a
    truncated read gets mistaken for a clean one."""
    cp = _bd(repo, *args)
    if cp.returncode != 0 or not cp.stdout.strip():
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(cp.stdout)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError):
        return None
    if isinstance(data, list):
        return data
    return [data]


# --- metadata declaration reading (NO prose fallback, by design) -----------

def declared_writes_from_metadata(metadata: dict) -> tuple[bool, frozenset]:
    """(declared, files).

    `declared` is False ONLY when NEITHER wip_claims NOR footprint_writes is
    a key in `metadata` at all — the distinct NO-DECLARATION case
    (SABLE-e2ic3 / SABLE-tm8nl). When either key IS present, even with an
    empty/blank value, `declared` is True: a present-but-empty declaration is
    a real "declares nothing" answer and must stay distinguishable from an
    absent one, the same trichotomy this codebase already applies elsewhere
    (sable_footprint_lib._metadata_entries). `files` is the union of
    whatever both keys parse to when more than one is present.

    There is deliberately NO fallback to parsing a bead's '## File
    footprint' prose section here — see the module docstring's "NO PROSE
    FALLBACK" section for why."""
    meta = metadata or {}
    present = [k for k in ("wip_claims", "footprint_writes") if k in meta]
    if not present:
        return False, frozenset()
    files: set[str] = set()
    for key in present:
        raw = meta.get(key) or ""
        files.update(p.strip() for p in raw.split(",") if p.strip())
    return True, frozenset(files)


# --- pure dispatch-screening logic -------------------------------------------

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ScreenVerdict:
    bead_id: str
    verdict: str
    declared_files: frozenset = frozenset()
    hits: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    sibling_hits: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    readiness: str = READY
    blockers: tuple = ()


def screen_candidate(bead_id: str, declared: bool, files: frozenset,
                      occupants: dict, sibling_hits: dict | None = None,
                      readiness: str = READY, blockers=()) -> ScreenVerdict:
    """The pure collision decision. `occupants` is {label: frozenset(paths)}
    built from BOTH occupant legs, already excluding this dispatch's own
    bead id(s) from leg (a). `sibling_hits` is leg (c) — {other_candidate_id:
    (shared paths,)} from sibling_overlaps().

    Verdict precedence on the footprint axis: NO-DECLARATION (nothing to
    check with) > COLLIDES (an occupant holds the file NOW) > SIBLING-
    COLLISION (only a co-candidate wants it) > CLEAR. A candidate that hits
    both an occupant and a sibling reads COLLIDES, and its sibling hits are
    still carried in `sibling_hits` and rendered — the stronger verdict wins
    the label, neither fact is discarded.

    `readiness`/`blockers` ride alongside and never change the footprint
    verdict: a blocked bead that genuinely does not overlap anything is
    honestly CLEAR *on files* and BLOCKED *on readiness*, and collapsing
    those into one word is the SABLE-qyr0f defect itself."""
    sibling_hits = dict(sibling_hits or {})
    if not declared:
        # No declaration means no operands — for the occupant legs AND for
        # the sibling leg. An undeclared pair is NOT a CLEAR pair
        # (SABLE-e2ic3): "nothing to compare" must not read as "compared,
        # found nothing".
        return ScreenVerdict(bead_id, NO_DECLARATION, readiness=readiness,
                             blockers=tuple(blockers))
    hits = {label: tuple(sorted(files & occ_files))
            for label, occ_files in occupants.items() if files & occ_files}
    if hits:
        verdict = COLLIDES
    elif sibling_hits:
        verdict = SIBLING_COLLISION
    else:
        verdict = CLEAR
    return ScreenVerdict(bead_id, verdict, files, hits, sibling_hits,
                         readiness, tuple(blockers))


def sibling_overlaps(declarations: dict) -> dict:
    """Occupant leg (c): candidate-vs-candidate (SABLE-1muvg).

    `declarations` is {candidate_id: frozenset(paths)} for the candidates
    that DECLARED something (undeclared candidates are absent from it — they
    have no operands, and inventing an empty set for them would let a pair
    of undeclared beads read as compared-and-clean). Returns
    {candidate_id: {sibling_id: (shared, paths)}}, entries only for
    candidates with at least one overlap.

    EVERY PAIR, not each-against-the-first: an implementation that compares
    candidate i only to candidate 0 passes a 2-candidate test and misses a
    3-candidate list whose 2nd and 3rd overlap. A repeated bead id collapses
    to one key here, so a bead can never collide with itself."""
    ids = list(declarations)
    out: dict[str, dict] = {bid: {} for bid in ids}
    for i, left in enumerate(ids):
        for right in ids[i + 1:]:
            shared = tuple(sorted(declarations[left] & declarations[right]))
            if shared:
                out[left][right] = shared
                out[right][left] = shared
    return {bid: hits for bid, hits in out.items() if hits}


def open_blocking_deps(bead: dict) -> tuple:
    """The precise readiness signal (SABLE-qyr0f): the ids of this bead's
    'blocks' dependencies that are not closed, read from the bead's own `bd
    show --json` record.

    Two record shapes exist and both are accepted — `bd show --json` resolves
    each dep to a full record ({'id', 'status', 'dependency_type'}) while `bd
    list --json` returns raw edges ({'depends_on_id', 'type'}) with no
    status. Status is read, never mere presence: a CLOSED blocker must not
    suppress CLEAR, and a screen that read dep presence instead of dep status
    would deny every bead that ever had a dependency."""
    blockers = []
    for dep in bead.get("dependencies") or []:
        if (dep.get("dependency_type") or dep.get("type")) not in BLOCKING_DEP_TYPES:
            continue
        dep_id = dep.get("id") or dep.get("depends_on_id")
        if not dep_id:
            continue
        if (dep.get("status") or "") in CLOSED_DEP_STATUSES:
            continue
        blockers.append(dep_id)
    return tuple(sorted(set(blockers)))


def readiness_for(bead: dict, ready_ids) -> tuple:
    """(readiness, blockers) for one candidate.

    `ready_ids` is the id set from `bd ready --limit 0`, or None when that
    query could not be read at all — None yields READINESS-UNKNOWN rather
    than a guess in either direction, because a probe that cannot fire is
    indistinguishable from a condition never met.

    BLOCKED (open 'blocks' dep) denies. NOT-READY (absent from the ready pool
    for some OTHER reason — already in_progress, closed) is reported with the
    status named but does not deny: absence from `bd ready` conflates
    blocked-by-dependency with already-claimed, and the two have different
    remedies."""
    blockers = open_blocking_deps(bead)
    if blockers:
        return BLOCKED, blockers
    if ready_ids is None:
        return READINESS_UNKNOWN, ()
    if (bead.get("id") or "") in ready_ids:
        return READY, ()
    return NOT_READY, ()


def ready_id_set(repo: str):
    """The id set from `bd ready --limit 0`, or None when unreadable.

    --limit 0 is MANDATORY: a bare `bd ready` truncates at 50 silently
    (SABLE-52aym) and a truncated ready set makes blocked and ready
    indistinguishable — which would fail in the RELEASING direction inside
    the fix for a releasing-direction bug."""
    rows = _bd_json(repo, "ready", "--json", "--limit", "0")
    if rows is None:
        return None
    return frozenset(r.get("id") for r in rows if r.get("id"))


def in_progress_occupants(beads: list, exclude_ids) -> dict:
    """Occupant leg (a): every OTHER in_progress bead's declared writes. A
    bead that itself declares nothing does not occupy anything — that is a
    fact about occupancy (nothing to collide with), orthogonal to whether a
    CANDIDATE declares nothing (which is reported, never silently CLEAR)."""
    exclude = set(exclude_ids)
    occ: dict[str, frozenset] = {}
    for b in beads:
        bid = b.get("id") or ""
        if not bid or bid in exclude:
            continue
        _, files = declared_writes_from_metadata(b.get("metadata") or {})
        if files:
            occ[f"in_progress:{bid}"] = files
    return occ


# --- git-plumbing primitives (each pinned by a unit test against real git) --

def resolve_ref(repo: str, ref: str) -> str:
    """`git rev-parse --verify --quiet "<ref>^{commit}"` -> "" when
    unresolvable, a commit SHA otherwise.

    THE FIX this whole bead exists for: the bare form `git rev-parse <ref>`
    ECHOES the literal ref string back to stdout when <ref> does not exist,
    reporting failure ONLY through the exit status. A caller that reads
    stdout as the answer without checking rc sees a non-empty, plausible
    string for a branch that landed and was reaped months ago. --verify
    --quiet instead answers with EMPTY stdout for an unresolvable ref, which
    is the only thing every caller in this file treats as "no such ref"."""
    cp = _git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{ref}^{{commit}}")
    return cp.stdout.strip() if cp.returncode == 0 else ""


def is_ancestor(repo: str, sha: str, ancestor_of: str):
    """`git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> <ancestor_of>` -> True (sha IS
    contained), False (sha is NOT contained), or None (unresolved — bad ref
    or git failure; never guessed at as either verdict). The argument order
    is fixed at this single call site rather than accepted from a caller, so
    the SABLE-gdp05 inversion class of bug has nowhere to enter here."""
    cp = _git(repo, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", sha, ancestor_of)
    if cp.returncode == 0:
        return True
    if cp.returncode == 1:
        return False
    return None


def branch_diff_files(repo: str, base: str, tip: str) -> frozenset:
    cp = _git(repo, "diff", "--name-only", base, tip)
    if cp.returncode != 0:
        return frozenset()
    return frozenset(p for p in cp.stdout.splitlines() if p.strip())


def wk_branch_occupants(repo: str, integration_ref: str) -> dict:
    """Occupant leg (b): every origin wk-* branch NOT contained in the
    integration branch, keyed by its ACTUAL git-diffed changed files —
    ground truth, never a declaration. This is the leg a declaration-only
    screen cannot see (SABLE-krbxd: undeclared collateral edits)."""
    occ: dict[str, frozenset] = {}
    refs_cp = _git(repo, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)",
                    "refs/remotes/origin/wk-*")
    if refs_cp.returncode != 0:
        return occ
    for ref in refs_cp.stdout.splitlines():
        ref = ref.strip()
        if not ref:
            continue
        tip = resolve_ref(repo, ref)
        if not tip:
            continue  # gone from origin between the listing and the probe
        contained = is_ancestor(repo, tip, integration_ref)
        if contained:
            continue  # landed — not an occupant
        base_cp = _git(repo, "merge-base", integration_ref, tip)
        base = base_cp.stdout.strip() if base_cp.returncode == 0 else integration_ref
        branch_name = ref[len("origin/"):] if ref.startswith("origin/") else ref
        files = branch_diff_files(repo, base, tip)
        if files:
            occ[f"branch:{branch_name}"] = files
    return occ


def hold_fields_state(metadatas: list) -> tuple:
    """(holders, best_missing, any_present) across every bead on ONE branch.

    Pure — no git. `holders` is the index list of beads carrying all four
    fields; the BRANCH is held when that list is non-empty (SABLE-jhako: a
    hold is placed once, on whichever bead the placer was shown, and the
    thing actually held is the branch). `best_missing` is what is still
    missing on the best-covered bead — the shortest real remedy, not the
    union across beads, which would ask a placer to re-add fields a sibling
    already carries. `any_present` is every hold field present anywhere on
    the branch, used only to spot a stale hold on a landed branch."""
    holders = []
    missing_sets = []
    present: set = set()
    for idx, meta in enumerate(metadatas):
        meta = meta or {}
        missing = tuple(f for f in HOLD_FIELDS if not meta.get(f))
        present.update(f for f in HOLD_FIELDS if meta.get(f))
        if missing:
            missing_sets.append(missing)
        else:
            holders.append(idx)
    best_missing = () if holders else min(
        missing_sets, key=lambda m: (len(m), m), default=HOLD_FIELDS)
    return holders, best_missing, tuple(f for f in HOLD_FIELDS if f in present)


def hold_verdict_for_branch(repo: str, integration_ref: str, branch: str,
                            metadatas: list):
    """(verdict, fields, holders) for ONE branch and every closed bead on it.

    verdict is one of REAPED_OR_UNPUSHED, NEEDS_HOLD, HELD_OK, LANDED_CLEAN,
    LANDED_STALE_HOLD, UNRESOLVED. `fields` is the missing-fields tuple for
    NEEDS_HOLD, the present-fields tuple for LANDED_STALE_HOLD, and empty
    otherwise. `holders` is the indexes of the beads that carry a complete
    hold.

    THE REAPED-BRANCH REGRESSION this function is the fix for: resolve_ref
    (not a bare rev-parse) decides whether the branch exists on origin at
    all, BEFORE any containment question is asked. A branch with no ref on
    origin is REAPED_OR_UNPUSHED — never NEEDS_HOLD — regardless of what its
    metadata does or does not carry."""
    tip = resolve_ref(repo, f"origin/{branch}")
    if not tip:
        return REAPED_OR_UNPUSHED, (), []
    contained = is_ancestor(repo, tip, integration_ref)
    if contained is None:
        return UNRESOLVED, (), []
    holders, missing, present = hold_fields_state(metadatas)
    if contained:
        return (LANDED_STALE_HOLD if present else LANDED_CLEAN), present, holders
    return (NEEDS_HOLD if missing else HELD_OK), missing, holders


def hold_verdict_for_bead(repo: str, integration_ref: str, branch: str,
                          metadata: dict):
    """(verdict, fields) for ONE bead's branch — the single-bead spelling of
    hold_verdict_for_branch, kept because a one-bead branch is exactly the
    case that must stay unchanged (SABLE-jhako's third control)."""
    verdict, fields_, _ = hold_verdict_for_branch(
        repo, integration_ref, branch, [metadata])
    return verdict, fields_


# =============================================================================
# ACTIVATION SHAPE — the landability screen (SABLE-6eu9w)
# =============================================================================

# Path-level activation classes. The RANK is the whole point: a bead's verdict
# is the worst case across its paths, because one hot-swap path holds the whole
# branch. COULD-NOT-ASSESS sits ABOVE the two inert-but-owing classes and BELOW
# hot-swap: an unknown must not be quietly reported as landable, but neither
# should it mask a hot-swap path we DID resolve.
HOT_SWAP = "hot-swap"
HOT_SWAP_CLOSURE = "hot-swap-closure"
PIN_MANAGED = "pin-managed"
COPY_INSTALLED = "copy-installed"
INERT = "inert"
PATH_UNASSESSED = "could-not-assess"

_ACTIVATION_RANK = {INERT: 0, COPY_INSTALLED: 1, PIN_MANAGED: 2,
                    PATH_UNASSESSED: 3, HOT_SWAP_CLOSURE: 4, HOT_SWAP: 5}

# Bead-level verdicts.
LANDABLE = "landable"
LANDABLE_OWES_ACTIVATION = "landable-owes-activation"
ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED = "could-not-assess"
HOLD_EXPECTED = "hold-expected"
NO_FOOTPRINT = "no-declaration"

_VERDICT_FOR_CLASS = {
    INERT: LANDABLE,
    COPY_INSTALLED: LANDABLE_OWES_ACTIVATION,
    PIN_MANAGED: LANDABLE_OWES_ACTIVATION,
    PATH_UNASSESSED: ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED,
    HOT_SWAP_CLOSURE: HOLD_EXPECTED,
    HOT_SWAP: HOLD_EXPECTED,
}

# Section states (the SABLE-47try trichotomy, kept as three inputs not two).
SECTION_ABSENT = "absent"
SECTION_UNREADABLE = "present-but-unreadable"
SECTION_EMPTY = "declared-empty"
SECTION_DECLARED = "declared"
SECTION_FIELD = "declared-field"


def _footprint_section(bead: dict) -> tuple:
    """(section_state, entries, unparsed) for ONE bead's declared writes.

    TOKENIZATION IS BORROWED, NEVER REBUILT. sable_footprint_lib is the single
    owner of footprint tokenization for this repo, and a screen carrying its
    own private token filter is the SABLE-ev5i5 three-mirror disease — the one
    that cost a full investigation on 2026-07-26. This deliberately consumes
    the lib's section reader instead of re-deriving a weaker one, so it
    inherits whatever that parser decides, including SABLE-g0elq's
    path_tokens() filter-plus-diagnostic once it lands.

    The names bound here are private today because the lib's only public
    footprint entry point, parse_declared_footprint(), discards the two facts
    this screen needs: whether the heading was present AT ALL (absent means
    "declares nothing", which is legitimate and must not read as a finding),
    and which tokens the parser could NOT recognise as paths. declared_reads()
    inside the lib reaches for _collect_section for exactly this reason and
    documents it; this is the same reach from one module out. The import is
    LATE so that a rename upstream breaks only this subcommand, loudly, instead
    of taking `dispatch` and `holds` — which never touch the lib — down with it.

    Structured-field precedence mirrors the lib's own declared_footprint():
    metadata.footprint_writes, when present, is used VERBATIM and the prose
    parser is not consulted. `bd show --json` carries that metadata; `bd ready
    --json` does not, so pool-wide screening runs on prose, which is precisely
    the input the phantom correction is about.
    """
    import sable_footprint_lib as fp_lib

    structured = fp_lib._metadata_entries(bead.get("metadata") or {},
                                          "footprint_writes")
    if structured is not None:
        state = SECTION_FIELD if structured else SECTION_EMPTY
        return state, frozenset(structured), frozenset()
    found, entries, dropped = fp_lib._collect_section(
        bead.get("description") or "", fp_lib._FOOTPRINT_HEADING)
    if not found:
        return SECTION_ABSENT, frozenset(), frozenset()
    if not entries:
        # Present, and yields no path token. Two sub-cases collapse here on
        # purpose: a deliberate lone 'none' marker (dropped is empty — a real
        # "declared, and empty" answer) and a prose paragraph the parser could
        # not read (dropped is not empty — SABLE-47try's unreadable state).
        return (SECTION_EMPTY if not dropped else SECTION_UNREADABLE), \
            frozenset(), dropped
    return SECTION_DECLARED, entries, dropped


# --- install-state resolution (resolved live, never enumerated) -------------

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class InstalledArtifact:
    """One thing an installer wrote into an install root."""
    path: str      # absolute path of the installed entry
    root: str      # the install root it was found under
    rel: str       # its path relative to that root
    target: str    # os.path.realpath(path) — where it ACTUALLY resolves
    linked: bool   # the entry (or a component of it) is a symlink


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class InstallScan:
    artifacts: tuple = ()
    scanned: tuple = ()      # roots that existed and were read completely
    unreadable: tuple = ()   # roots that exist but could not be fully read
    absent: tuple = ()       # roots that are simply not there


def default_install_roots() -> tuple:
    """Where THIS methodology's installers write — sable-bin-install's DEST
    (~/.local/bin) and install.sh's destinations under CLAUDE_DIR.

    This is a list of DESTINATIONS, not a classification: it says where to
    LOOK, and every verdict is still resolved from what is actually found
    there. That distinction is the whole SABLE-hadqx line — a hardcoded list
    of which bins are symlinks rots the moment someone pins one; a list of
    directories installers write to changes only when the installers do.
    CLAUDE_DIR's subdirectories are named rather than walking CLAUDE_DIR
    itself, which also holds ~/.claude/projects and the .install-bak-* trees —
    tens of thousands of files that no installer sourced from this repo."""
    home = os.path.expanduser("~")
    claude = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or os.path.join(home, ".claude")
    return (
        os.path.join(home, ".local", "bin"),
        os.path.join(claude, "hooks"),
        os.path.join(claude, "agents"),
        os.path.join(claude, "skills"),
        os.path.join(claude, "commands"),
        os.path.join(claude, "bin"),
    )


def configured_install_roots(override=None) -> tuple:
    """CLI flag > SABLE_SCREEN_INSTALL_ROOTS > defaults.

    An env var set to the EMPTY string yields no roots, which this reports as
    a scan with nothing scanned — i.e. COULD-NOT-ASSESS, not "nothing is
    installed". A test that wants "nothing is installed" points the seam at an
    empty scratch directory, which is a real (and readable) answer."""
    if override:
        return tuple(override)
    raw = os.environ.get("SABLE_SCREEN_INSTALL_ROOTS")
    if raw is not None:
        return tuple(p for p in raw.split(os.pathsep) if p.strip())
    return default_install_roots()


def scan_install_roots(roots) -> InstallScan:
    """Walk every install root and record what is there, with each entry
    resolved through readlink -f (os.path.realpath).

    A root is walked at its REALPATH so that a symlinked ancestor (e.g. a
    /home that is itself a link) does not make every entry beneath it look
    linked. Symlinked DIRECTORIES are recorded as artifacts in their own right
    and not descended into: a skill directory linked into the repo is one
    hot-swap artifact, not a hundred.

    A root that does not exist is `absent` — an installer that was never run
    contributes nothing and that is a real answer. A root that exists but
    cannot be fully read is `unreadable`, which the caller turns into
    COULD-NOT-ASSESS: an unreadable install prefix is NOT "nothing is
    installed" (the standing silent-instrument contract)."""
    artifacts: list = []
    scanned: list = []
    unreadable: list = []
    absent: list = []
    for raw_root in roots:
        root = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(raw_root))
        if not os.path.isdir(root):
            absent.append(root)
            continue
        errors: list = []
        try:
            for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(
                    root, followlinks=False, onerror=errors.append):
                linked_dirs = [d for d in dirnames
                               if os.path.islink(os.path.join(dirpath, d))]
                for d in linked_dirs:
                    dirnames.remove(d)
                for name in filenames + linked_dirs:
                    path = os.path.join(dirpath, name)
                    target = os.path.realpath(path)
                    artifacts.append(InstalledArtifact(
                        path=path, root=root,
                        rel=os.path.relpath(path, root),
                        target=target,
                        linked=target != path))
        except OSError as exc:               # pragma: no cover - defensive
            errors.append(exc)
        (unreadable if errors else scanned).append(root)
    return InstallScan(tuple(artifacts), tuple(scanned), tuple(unreadable),
                       tuple(absent))


def worktree_roots(repo: str):
    """Every live working tree of THIS repo, from `git worktree list
    --porcelain` — or None when git cannot answer.

    This is what makes HOT-SWAP mechanical. The installed symlinks point at
    whichever tree the operator installed from (usually the main worktree, not
    the caller's), so "resolves into the working tree" can only be decided
    against the FULL set of trees. None (git failed, or the answer was empty)
    is a non-answer, never a guess: silently missing the main worktree would
    reclassify every live symlink as PIN-MANAGED, i.e. downgrade a hold to a
    landable — the one direction this screen must not fail in."""
    cp = _git(repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain")
    if cp.returncode != 0:
        return None
    roots = [os.path.realpath(line[len("worktree "):].strip())
             for line in cp.stdout.splitlines()
             if line.startswith("worktree ")]
    return tuple(dict.fromkeys(roots)) or None


def _under_any(path: str, roots) -> bool:
    return any(path == r or path.startswith(r.rstrip(os.sep) + os.sep)
               for r in roots)


def artifact_class(art: InstalledArtifact, tree_roots) -> str:
    """The activation shape of ONE installed artifact.

    Resolved, in this order, from facts about the artifact itself:
      resolves INTO a live working tree  -> HOT-SWAP     (the pull activates)
      resolves elsewhere, via a link     -> PIN-MANAGED  (a re-pin activates)
      is a real file                     -> COPY-INSTALLED (an install activates)
    PIN-MANAGED is defined as "linked, but not into a live tree" rather than
    "under ~/.local/lib/sable-<sha>/", so it needs no knowledge of the pinning
    layout and survives that layout changing. Note gate-closure membership is a
    SEPARATE question from being pinned, and is not answered here."""
    if _under_any(art.target, tree_roots):
        return HOT_SWAP
    return PIN_MANAGED if art.linked else COPY_INSTALLED


# --- the live import closure (SABLE-9boz4's hazard, read forwards) ----------

# Deliberately NARROW. Only an IMPORT or a SOURCE statement counts, never a
# mention: a docstring naming a test file is not a dependency, and the loose
# "same directory" and "appears anywhere in the text" rules were both measured
# against this repo's bin/ before being rejected — same-directory pulls in every
# test file (making every bin/ bead read HOLD-EXPECTED, which destroys the
# negative control), and word-mention pulls in 6 test files that live bins merely
# name in prose. The narrow rule adds exactly the 23 sibling libraries and zero
# test files.
_PY_IMPORT = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*(?:from|import)[ \t]+([A-Za-z_]\w*)", re.M)
_SH_SOURCE = re.compile(
    r"(?:^|[ \t;])(?:source|\.)[ \t]+[^\n]*?([\w.-]+\.(?:sh|bash))", re.M)

# A file bigger than this is not a script this closure is about; skipping it
# bounds the read cost of a directory that happens to hold a large artifact.
_CLOSURE_MAX_BYTES = 512 * 1024


class ClosureIndex:
    """Which repo files are ALREADY LIVE because a hot-swap artifact imports
    them (SABLE-6eu9w, found while smoke-testing this screen against the real
    install state).

    A hot-swap symlink activates its whole IMPORT CLOSURE, not just the one
    file the symlink names — sable-bin-install's own docstring records the
    other half of this (copying just the entry point severs the sibling
    imports, which broke a smoke test and was rolled back live). So a bead
    touching only bin/sable_footprint_lib.py — which no installer links, and
    which therefore matches no artifact — would read LANDABLE while in fact
    sable-spawn-worker imports it through a live symlink and the branch is
    held. That is the SAME permissive direction the phantom correction is
    about, reached from the other side: a file can activate on pull without
    being installed.

    The closure is RESOLVED, not listed: the seeds are wherever hot-swap
    targets actually landed, and the edges are read out of the files
    themselves at screen time. Scope is one directory — sibling imports are
    how every seed in this repo reaches its libraries (Python inserts the
    script's own dir on sys.path; the shell hooks source `$(dirname)/lib-*.sh`)
    — and it is transitive within it, so a lib reached only through another
    lib is still caught."""

    def __init__(self, repo_root: str, seeds: dict):
        self.repo_root = repo_root
        self.seeds = seeds          # repo-relative dir -> {basename, ...}
        self._cache: dict = {}

    def members(self, reldir: str) -> frozenset:
        if reldir not in self._cache:
            self._cache[reldir] = self._compute(reldir)
        return self._cache[reldir]

    def _compute(self, reldir: str) -> frozenset:
        seeds = self.seeds.get(reldir)
        if not seeds:
            return frozenset()
        directory = os.path.join(self.repo_root, reldir) if reldir else self.repo_root
        texts: dict = {}
        try:
            names = os.listdir(directory)
        except OSError:
            return frozenset()
        for name in names:
            path = os.path.join(directory, name)
            try:
                if not os.path.isfile(path) or os.path.getsize(path) > _CLOSURE_MAX_BYTES:
                    continue
                with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                    texts[name] = fh.read()
            except OSError:
                continue
        closure = set(seeds)
        frontier = set(seeds)
        while frontier:
            nxt: set = set()
            for name in frontier:
                for ref in self._refs(texts.get(name, ""), texts):
                    if ref not in closure:
                        nxt.add(ref)
            closure |= nxt
            frontier = nxt
        return frozenset(closure - set(seeds))

    @staticmethod
    def _refs(text: str, texts: dict) -> set:
        out = set()
        for module in _PY_IMPORT.findall(text):
            if module + ".py" in texts:
                out.add(module + ".py")
        for name in _SH_SOURCE.findall(text):
            if name in texts:
                out.add(name)
        return out


def hot_swap_seeds(scan: InstallScan, tree_roots) -> dict:
    """{repo-relative dir: {basename, ...}} for every artifact that resolves
    into a live tree — the closure's seeds, read off the resolved targets so
    they move when the install layout does."""
    seeds: dict = {}
    for art in scan.artifacts:
        if artifact_class(art, tree_roots) != HOT_SWAP:
            continue
        root = next((r for r in tree_roots if _under_any(art.target, [r])), None)
        if root is None:                     # pragma: no cover - defensive
            continue
        rel = os.path.relpath(art.target, root)
        seeds.setdefault(os.path.dirname(rel), set()).add(os.path.basename(rel))
    return seeds


def _rel_suffix_match(repo_rel: str, install_rel: str) -> bool:
    """Does an artifact installed at <root>/<install_rel> correspond to the
    repo path <repo_rel>?

    Matched on a whole-component suffix, which covers both install layouts
    without naming either: the flat one (bin/sable-doctor -> ~/.local/bin/
    sable-doctor) and the mirrored one (hooks/multi-manager/x.sh ->
    ~/.claude/hooks/multi-manager/x.sh). It is the ONLY way a PIN-MANAGED or
    COPY-INSTALLED artifact can be attributed to a repo path at all: those do
    not resolve back into the tree, so exact-target matching cannot see them.
    A false match here over-reports (a copy owing an install), never under."""
    if not install_rel or install_rel.startswith(".."):
        return False
    return repo_rel == install_rel or repo_rel.endswith("/" + install_rel)


def classify_repo_path(rel: str, repo_root: str, scan: InstallScan,
                       tree_roots, closure: "ClosureIndex | None" = None) -> tuple:
    """(klass, evidence, reason) for ONE repo-relative path.

    An artifact is attributed to this path either because it RESOLVES to it
    (the same repo-relative path under any live tree — this is the leg that
    catches hot-swaps) or because its installed-relative path is a
    whole-component suffix of it (the leg that catches pins and copies).

    A path that does not EXIST in the repo contributes COULD-NOT-ASSESS
    (SABLE-35mqf's gate): it is either a phantom scraped out of prose or a
    file the bead will create, and both need saying out loud. Reporting it
    INERT is the permissive failure the hand-run made — a phantom matches no
    installed artifact, so it would push the bead toward the landable list.
    Because the classes are RANKED rather than sequenced, a dangling installed
    symlink pointing at a not-yet-existing path still reads HOT-SWAP: the fact
    we resolved outranks the fact we could not."""
    if os.path.isabs(rel) or rel.startswith("../") or not rel:
        return PATH_UNASSESSED, (), "not a repo-relative path"
    candidates = {os.path.realpath(os.path.join(root, rel))
                  for root in tree_roots}
    hits = [a for a in scan.artifacts
            if a.target in candidates or _rel_suffix_match(rel, a.rel)]
    classes = [(artifact_class(a, tree_roots), a) for a in hits]
    exists = os.path.exists(os.path.join(repo_root, rel))
    if not exists:
        classes.append((PATH_UNASSESSED, None))
    if closure is not None and os.path.basename(rel) in closure.members(
            os.path.dirname(rel)):
        classes.append((HOT_SWAP_CLOSURE, None))
    if not classes:
        return INERT, (), "no installed artifact resolves to it"
    klass = max((k for k, _ in classes), key=lambda k: _ACTIVATION_RANK[k])
    evidence = tuple(sorted(a.path for k, a in classes
                            if a is not None and k == klass))
    reason = {
        HOT_SWAP: "installed artifact resolves into a live working tree — "
                  "the pull IS the activation",
        HOT_SWAP_CLOSURE: "not installed itself, but imported/sourced by a "
                          "hot-swap artifact beside it — the pull activates "
                          "the whole import closure",
        PIN_MANAGED: "installed as a symlink into a pinned snapshot — lands "
                     "inert; a re-pin activates",
        COPY_INSTALLED: "installed as a real file — lands inert; an install "
                        "activates (obligation has no ledger, SABLE-3zjc1)",
        PATH_UNASSESSED: "no such path in the repo — phantom token or a file "
                         "this bead creates; NOT assumed inert",
    }[klass]
    return klass, evidence, reason


def expand_entry(repo_root: str, entry: str) -> tuple:
    """(paths, resolvable) for one declared entry.

    A concrete path expands to itself. A directory entry (the trailing-slash
    form only planner-declared footprints produce) expands to every file
    beneath it, so a declared directory is classified by what is actually IN
    it rather than by a guess about the directory as a whole."""
    rel = entry.rstrip("/")
    # An absolute or escaping entry is not resolvable AND must not be walked:
    # expanding '../' would classify files outside the repo entirely.
    if os.path.isabs(rel) or not rel or rel.startswith("../") or rel == "..":
        return (rel,), False
    target = os.path.join(repo_root, rel)
    if os.path.isdir(target):
        out = []
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(target):
            dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d != ".git"]
            for name in filenames:
                out.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, name),
                                           repo_root))
        return tuple(sorted(out)), True
    return (rel,), True


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ActivationVerdict:
    bead_id: str
    verdict: str
    section: str
    paths: tuple = ()        # ({"path", "class", "installed", "reason"}, ...)
    unparsed: tuple = ()
    note: str = ""
    title: str = ""


def activation_for_bead(bead: dict, repo_root: str, scan: InstallScan,
                        tree_roots, blocked: str = "",
                        closure: "ClosureIndex | None" = None) -> ActivationVerdict:
    """The whole landability story for ONE bead.

    `blocked` is a non-empty reason when the install state itself could not be
    resolved (no readable install root, or git could not enumerate the
    worktrees). Every DECLARED bead then reads COULD-NOT-ASSESS — a broken
    instrument reports that it is broken rather than reporting that nothing
    activates. A bead that declares nothing is unaffected: there was nothing
    to assess either way, which is why NO-DECLARATION is its own verdict and
    not a flavour of landable."""
    bead_id = bead.get("id") or ""
    title = (bead.get("title") or "")[:80]
    section, entries, unparsed = _footprint_section(bead)
    if section == SECTION_ABSENT:
        return ActivationVerdict(
            bead_id, NO_FOOTPRINT, section, (), (),
            "declares no footprint — nothing to assess, dispatches normally",
            title)
    if blocked:
        return ActivationVerdict(bead_id, ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED, section,
                                 (), tuple(sorted(unparsed)), blocked, title)

    rows = []
    ranks = []
    for entry in sorted(entries):
        expanded, resolvable = expand_entry(repo_root, entry)
        if not resolvable:
            rows.append({"path": entry, "class": PATH_UNASSESSED,
                         "installed": [],
                         "reason": "not a repo-relative path"})
            ranks.append(PATH_UNASSESSED)
            continue
        for rel in expanded:
            klass, evidence, reason = classify_repo_path(
                rel, repo_root, scan, tree_roots, closure)
            rows.append({"path": rel, "class": klass,
                         "installed": list(evidence), "reason": reason})
            ranks.append(klass)
    if unparsed:
        # A token the borrowed tokenizer could not recognise as a path is a
        # partially-understood declaration, and the write side has no
        # mechanical fallback here the way sable_footprint_lib's does. Same
        # ruling as SABLE-zx2yv on the read side: present-but-incomplete is
        # not "declared, and complete".
        ranks.append(PATH_UNASSESSED)
    if not ranks:
        # Declared, explicitly empty. A real answer ("this activates on
        # nothing"), distinct from an absent section.
        return ActivationVerdict(bead_id, LANDABLE, section, (), (),
                                 "declared an empty footprint", title)
    worst = max(ranks, key=lambda k: _ACTIVATION_RANK[k])
    verdict = _VERDICT_FOR_CLASS[worst]
    note = {
        HOLD_EXPECTED: "landing ACTIVATES an installed artifact — expect a "
                       "hold for activation shape, not for any defect",
        LANDABLE_OWES_ACTIVATION: "lands inert, but owes an activation step "
                                  "(re-pin / install) to take effect",
        ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED: "at least one declared token could not be "
                               "classified — not assumed inert",
        LANDABLE: "every declared path is inert — lands and takes effect with "
                  "no activation step",
    }[verdict]
    return ActivationVerdict(bead_id, verdict, section, tuple(rows),
                             tuple(sorted(unparsed)), note, title)


def scan_problem(scan: InstallScan, tree_roots) -> str:
    """The instrument's own health, as a reason string ("" when healthy)."""
    if scan.unreadable:
        return ("install root(s) present but unreadable: "
                f"{', '.join(scan.unreadable)} — an unreadable prefix is NOT "
                "'nothing is installed'")
    if not scan.scanned:
        return ("no readable install root "
                f"(absent: {', '.join(scan.absent) or 'none configured'}) — "
                "cannot tell an uninstalled repo from an unresolvable one")
    if not tree_roots:
        return ("`git worktree list` could not enumerate this repo's working "
                "trees — hot-swap and pin-managed are indistinguishable "
                "without them")
    return ""


# --- bd-touching orchestration ------------------------------------------------

def build_occupants(repo: str, integration_ref: str, exclude_ids) -> dict:
    occ: dict[str, frozenset] = {}
    in_progress = _bd_json(repo, "list", "--status", "in_progress", "--json",
                          "--limit", "0") or []
    occ.update(in_progress_occupants(in_progress, exclude_ids))
    occ.update(wk_branch_occupants(repo, integration_ref))
    return occ


def run_dispatch(repo: str, bead_ids: list) -> tuple[list, str]:
    integration_branch = git_lib.resolve_integration_branch(repo)
    integration_ref = f"origin/{integration_branch}"
    occupants = build_occupants(repo, integration_ref, set(bead_ids))
    ready_ids = ready_id_set(repo)

    # Read every candidate FIRST: leg (c) is a property of the candidate SET,
    # so no candidate can be decided until all of them have been read. A
    # repeated id collapses to one entry here, which is what stops leg (c)
    # from reporting a bead as colliding with itself.
    beads: dict[str, dict] = {}
    results = []
    for bid in bead_ids:
        record = _bd_json(repo, "show", bid, "--json")
        if not record:
            results.append({"bead_id": bid, "verdict": "not-found",
                            "declared_files": [], "hits": {},
                            "sibling_hits": {}, "readiness": READINESS_UNKNOWN,
                            "blockers": []})
            continue
        beads[bid] = record[0]

    declarations = {}
    for bid, bead in beads.items():
        declared, files = declared_writes_from_metadata(bead.get("metadata") or {})
        if declared:
            declarations[bid] = files
    sibling_hits = sibling_overlaps(declarations)

    for bid, bead in beads.items():
        declared, files = declared_writes_from_metadata(bead.get("metadata") or {})
        readiness, blockers = readiness_for(bead, ready_ids)
        verdict = screen_candidate(bid, declared, files, occupants,
                                    sibling_hits.get(bid), readiness, blockers)
        results.append({
            "bead_id": bid,
            "title": (bead.get("title") or "")[:80],
            "verdict": verdict.verdict,
            "declared_files": sorted(verdict.declared_files),
            "hits": verdict.hits,
            "sibling_hits": verdict.sibling_hits,
            "readiness": verdict.readiness,
            "blockers": list(verdict.blockers),
            "status": bead.get("status") or "",
        })
    # not-found rows were appended during the read pass; restore the order the
    # caller asked in so a reader can match rows to argv positionally.
    order = {bid: i for i, bid in enumerate(bead_ids)}
    results.sort(key=lambda r: order.get(r["bead_id"], len(order)))
    return results, integration_ref


def dispatch_exit_code(results: list) -> int:
    """0 every candidate is dispatchable, 1 at least one deny, 2 a candidate
    could not be read at all.

    A deny is COLLIDES, SIBLING-COLLISION, or BLOCKED — three different facts
    that all mean "do not dispatch this set as asked". NO-DECLARATION and
    NOT-READY are reported and still release: a screen that can never release
    is exactly as broken as one that can never deny."""
    if any(r["verdict"] == "not-found" for r in results):
        return 2
    denied = {COLLIDES, SIBLING_COLLISION}
    if any(r["verdict"] in denied or r.get("readiness") == BLOCKED
           for r in results):
        return 1
    return 0


def group_by_branch(beads: list) -> dict:
    """{branch: [bead, ...]} in first-seen order, skipping beads with no
    metadata.branch. The grouping IS the SABLE-jhako fix: it is what makes
    one branch produce one row instead of one row per bundled bead."""
    groups: dict[str, list] = {}
    for b in beads:
        branch = (b.get("metadata") or {}).get("branch")
        if not branch:
            continue
        groups.setdefault(branch, []).append(b)
    return groups


def run_holds(repo: str) -> tuple[list, str]:
    integration_branch = git_lib.resolve_integration_branch(repo)
    integration_ref = f"origin/{integration_branch}"
    closed = _bd_json(repo, "list", "--status", "closed", "--json",
                      "--limit", "0") or []

    results = []
    for branch, group in group_by_branch(closed).items():
        metas = [b.get("metadata") or {} for b in group]
        ids = [b.get("id") for b in group]
        verdict, fields_, holders = hold_verdict_for_branch(
            repo, integration_ref, branch, metas)
        results.append({
            "branch": branch,
            "beads": ids,
            # kept so a reader keyed on a single id still resolves; with one
            # bead on the branch this row is byte-identical to the old one.
            "bead_id": ids[0] if ids else None,
            "verdict": verdict,
            "fields": list(fields_),
            "held_by": [ids[i] for i in holders],
        })
    return results, integration_ref


def run_activation(repo: str, bead_ids: list, ready: bool = False,
                   roots=None) -> tuple:
    """(results, meta) for the activation screen.

    ONE bulk `bd ready --json --limit 0` for the pool form, and one `bd show`
    per explicitly named bead. The first hand-run of this screen called a
    per-bead footprint reader and shelled bd ~460 times; it did not finish
    inside two minutes. Parsing is in-process for exactly that reason — and the
    install state is scanned ONCE for the whole invocation, not per bead.

    --limit 0 is mandatory here for the same reason it is everywhere else in
    this file (SABLE-52aym: a bare `bd ready` truncates at 50 SILENTLY), and it
    fails in the releasing direction: beads past the cap would simply never be
    screened, and a pool screen that quietly covers the first 50 of 486 looks
    exactly like one that covered them all."""
    scan = scan_install_roots(configured_install_roots(roots))
    trees = worktree_roots(repo) or ()
    blocked = scan_problem(scan, trees)
    repo_root = os.path.realpath(repo)
    closure = ClosureIndex(repo_root, hot_swap_seeds(scan, trees))

    results = []
    seen = set()
    beads = []
    for bid in bead_ids:
        if bid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(bid)
        record = _bd_json(repo, "show", bid, "--json")
        if not record:
            results.append({"bead_id": bid, "verdict": "not-found",
                            "section": "", "paths": [], "unparsed": [],
                            "note": "bead could not be read", "title": ""})
            continue
        beads.append(record[0])
    if ready:
        rows = _bd_json(repo, "ready", "--json", "--limit", "0")
        if rows is None:
            results.append({"bead_id": "--ready", "verdict": "not-found",
                            "section": "", "paths": [], "unparsed": [],
                            "note": "`bd ready --json --limit 0` unreadable",
                            "title": ""})
            rows = []
        for row in rows:
            if (row.get("id") or "") in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(row.get("id") or "")
            beads.append(row)

    for bead in beads:
        v = activation_for_bead(bead, repo_root, scan, trees, blocked, closure)
        results.append({"bead_id": v.bead_id, "title": v.title,
                        "verdict": v.verdict, "section": v.section,
                        "paths": [dict(p) for p in v.paths],
                        "unparsed": list(v.unparsed), "note": v.note})
    meta = {
        "install_roots_scanned": list(scan.scanned),
        "install_roots_absent": list(scan.absent),
        "install_roots_unreadable": list(scan.unreadable),
        "installed_artifacts": len(scan.artifacts),
        "worktrees": list(trees),
        "blocked": blocked,
    }
    return results, meta


def activation_exit_code(results: list) -> int:
    """2 could-not-assess / unreadable bead, 1 hold-expected, else 0.

    2 OUTRANKS 1 deliberately. Both are non-zero, so a caller gating on success
    refuses either way; the difference is what a human does next. "The screen
    did its job and the answer is no" is actionable (bundle it into an operator
    window). "The screen could not do its job" is not, and must not be able to
    hide behind a verdict that looks decided."""
    if any(r["verdict"] in (ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED, "not-found")
           for r in results):
        return 2
    if any(r["verdict"] == HOLD_EXPECTED for r in results):
        return 1
    return 0


# --- rendering ----------------------------------------------------------------

_DISPATCH_ICON = {NO_DECLARATION: "⚠", CLEAR: "✓", COLLIDES: "✗",
                  SIBLING_COLLISION: "⇄"}


def render_dispatch_text(results: list, integration_ref: str) -> str:
    lines = [f"integration ref: {integration_ref}", ""]
    for r in results:
        if r["verdict"] == "not-found":
            lines.append(f"{r['bead_id']}: NOT FOUND")
            lines.append("")
            continue
        icon = _DISPATCH_ICON[r["verdict"]]
        lines.append(f"{icon} {r['bead_id']} [{r['verdict'].upper()}] {r.get('title', '')}")
        if r["verdict"] == NO_DECLARATION:
            lines.append("    NO DECLARED FOOTPRINT (SABLE-e2ic3: distinct from CLEAR)")
        elif r["declared_files"]:
            lines.append(f"    declares: {', '.join(r['declared_files'])}")
        for label, shared in r.get("hits", {}).items():
            lines.append(f"    ↳ {label}: {', '.join(shared)}")
        for sibling, shared in sorted(r.get("sibling_hits", {}).items()):
            lines.append(f"    ⇄ co-candidate {sibling}: {', '.join(shared)}")
        if r.get("sibling_hits"):
            lines.append("      remedy: bundle onto ONE worktree "
                          "(sable-spawn-worker <lead> --bundle <sibling>) or "
                          "dispatch them one after the other — do NOT give "
                          "them separate panes")
        lines.extend(_render_readiness(r))
        lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def _render_readiness(r: dict) -> list:
    """The readiness axis prints on its OWN line, below the footprint verdict
    (SABLE-qyr0f). A READY candidate prints nothing extra, which is what
    keeps the ordinary single-candidate output unchanged."""
    readiness = r.get("readiness", READY)
    if readiness == BLOCKED:
        return [f"    ⛔ BLOCKED: open blocking dependency "
                f"{', '.join(r.get('blockers') or [])} — not dispatchable "
                f"(readiness is a SEPARATE axis from the footprint verdict "
                f"above)"]
    if readiness == NOT_READY:
        status = r.get("status") or "unknown"
        return [f"    ⚠ not in the ready pool (status: {status}) — no open "
                f"blocking dep, so this is 'not dispatchable NOW', not "
                f"'blocked'"]
    if readiness == READINESS_UNKNOWN:
        return ["    ⚠ READINESS UNKNOWN: `bd ready --limit 0` unreadable; "
                "the footprint verdict above stands alone"]
    return []


_ACTIVATION_ICON = {HOLD_EXPECTED: "✗", LANDABLE: "✓",
                    LANDABLE_OWES_ACTIVATION: "◐",
                    ACTIVATION_UNASSESSED: "?", NO_FOOTPRINT: "⚠"}


def render_activation_text(results: list, meta: dict) -> str:
    """Text form. A LANDABLE bead prints ONE line and no paths — the negative
    control has to be quiet in the OUTPUT too, not merely in the exit code, or
    the screen becomes the wall of text managers learn to skip past."""
    lines = [
        f"install roots: {', '.join(meta['install_roots_scanned']) or '(none)'}"
        f"  [{meta['installed_artifacts']} artifact(s)]",
        f"live working trees: {len(meta['worktrees'])}",
    ]
    if meta["install_roots_unreadable"]:
        lines.append("UNREADABLE install root(s): "
                     f"{', '.join(meta['install_roots_unreadable'])}")
    if meta.get("blocked"):
        lines.append(f"*** INSTRUMENT DEGRADED: {meta['blocked']} ***")
    lines.append("")

    for r in results:
        icon = _ACTIVATION_ICON.get(r["verdict"], "?")
        lines.append(f"{icon} {r['bead_id']} [{r['verdict'].upper()}] "
                     f"{r.get('title', '')}".rstrip())
        if r["verdict"] in (LANDABLE, NO_FOOTPRINT):
            lines.append(f"    {r['note']}")
            lines.append("")
            continue
        by_class: dict = {}
        for p in r.get("paths") or []:
            by_class.setdefault(p["class"], []).append(p)
        for klass in sorted(by_class, key=lambda k: -_ACTIVATION_RANK[k]):
            for p in by_class[klass]:
                installed = (" ← " + ", ".join(p["installed"])
                             if p["installed"] else "")
                lines.append(f"    {klass:>15s}  {p['path']}{installed}")
            if klass != INERT:
                lines.append(f"    {'':>15s}  ({by_class[klass][0]['reason']})")
        if r.get("unparsed"):
            lines.append(f"    {'unparsed token':>15s}  "
                         f"{', '.join(r['unparsed'])}")
            lines.append(f"    {'':>15s}  (not recognised as a path — the "
                         f"declaration is only partly understood, so it is "
                         f"NOT reported inert)")
        lines.append(f"    → {r['note']}")
        lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def render_holds_text(results: list, integration_ref: str) -> str:
    needs = [r for r in results if r["verdict"] == NEEDS_HOLD]
    other = [r for r in results if r["verdict"] != NEEDS_HOLD]
    bead_count = sum(len(r.get("beads") or []) for r in results)
    lines = [
        f"integration ref: {integration_ref}",
        # rows are BRANCHES now (SABLE-jhako), so both counts are printed:
        # bundled branches make them differ, and the difference is the thing
        # the old bead-keyed sweep could not see.
        f"branches with a closed bead: {len(results)} "
        f"(across {bead_count} closed bead(s))",
        "",
        f"*** NEEDS HOLD ({len(needs)}) ***",
    ]
    for r in needs:
        lines.append(f"  {r['branch']:32s} missing: {','.join(r['fields'])}"
                      f"  beads: {', '.join(b for b in r['beads'] if b)}")
    if not needs:
        lines.append("  none")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(f"other ({len(other)}):")
    for r in other:
        note = ""
        if r["verdict"] == LANDED_STALE_HOLD:
            note = f" (landed but still holds {','.join(r['fields'])})"
        elif r["verdict"] == HELD_OK and r.get("held_by"):
            note = f" (hold carried by {', '.join(r['held_by'])})"
        lines.append(f"  {r['branch']:32s} {r['verdict']}{note}"
                      f"  beads: {', '.join(b for b in r['beads'] if b)}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


# --- CLI -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="sable-screen",
        description="The two manager pre-dispatch/pre-close safety screens, "
                     "mechanized so they survive a recycle (SABLE-23upx).",
    )
    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    d = sub.add_parser("dispatch", help="pre-dispatch collision screen")
    d.add_argument("bead_ids", nargs="+", metavar="BEAD-ID")
    d.add_argument("--repo", default=os.getcwd())
    d.add_argument("--format", choices=["text", "json"], default="text")

    h = sub.add_parser("holds", help="pre-close hold sweep")
    h.add_argument("--repo", default=os.getcwd())
    h.add_argument("--format", choices=["text", "json"], default="text")

    a = sub.add_parser("activation",
                       help="pre-dispatch landability screen (SABLE-6eu9w)")
    a.add_argument("bead_ids", nargs="*", metavar="BEAD-ID")
    a.add_argument("--ready", action="store_true",
                   help="also screen every bead in `bd ready --limit 0`")
    a.add_argument("--install-root", action="append", dest="install_roots",
                   metavar="DIR",
                   help="install root to resolve against (repeatable); "
                        "overrides SABLE_SCREEN_INSTALL_ROOTS")
    a.add_argument("--repo", default=os.getcwd())
    a.add_argument("--format", choices=["text", "json"], default="text")

    return parser


def main(argv: list | None = None) -> int:
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    repo = os.path.abspath(args.repo)

    if args.cmd == "activation":
        if not args.bead_ids and not args.ready:
            print("sable-screen activation: name at least one bead id, or "
                  "pass --ready to screen the whole ready pool",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        results, meta = run_activation(repo, args.bead_ids, args.ready,
                                       args.install_roots)
        if args.format == "json":
            print(json.dumps({"meta": meta, "results": results}, indent=2))
        else:
            print(render_activation_text(results, meta))
        return activation_exit_code(results)

    if args.cmd == "dispatch":
        results, integration_ref = run_dispatch(repo, args.bead_ids)
        if args.format == "json":
            print(json.dumps({"integration_ref": integration_ref, "results": results}, indent=2))
        else:
            print(render_dispatch_text(results, integration_ref))
        return dispatch_exit_code(results)

    results, integration_ref = run_holds(repo)
    if args.format == "json":
        print(json.dumps({"integration_ref": integration_ref, "results": results}, indent=2))
    else:
        print(render_holds_text(results, integration_ref))
    return 1 if any(r["verdict"] == NEEDS_HOLD for r in results) else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
